some people argue that individuals in  the United States making 250,000 dollars  a year  belong to the middle class others  disagree but the term middle class is  ambiguous what it means depends on who  you ask so let's ask what are some ways  to define the middle class a 2010  international study defined the global  middle class as households that spend  between 10 and $100 per person per day  but this definition becomes skewed in  more wealthier poor nations India for  instance considers their middle class to  be households that spend at least four  dollars a day here in the US an average  American spends closer to 95 dollars a  day companies often define the middle  class by looking at purchasing power in  developing countries like India or China  the middle class often refers to people  who can afford appliances in wealthy  countries the middle class can afford  larger purchases like cars since the  1960s the United Kingdom has measured a  social grade not by income but by  occupation a skilled manual worker is  still seen as working class while a  clerk is middle class even though the  manual worker may be making several  times with the clerk takes home in the  United States where class is usually  defined by money the median income is  about 52 thousand dollars a year  definitions like these don't indicate  that a two hundred and fifty thousand  dollar a year earner would qualify as  middle-class but according to a 2010 BDO  study between mortgages taxes and child  care an average family of four in  Huntington New York would actually incur  two hundred and seventy seven thousand  dollars worth of expenses effectively  going into the red on only two hundred  and fifty thousand dollars a year the  middle class is an effective talking  point for politicians and economists  because it's so malleable but for  settling debates about specific numbers  this ambiguous phrase leaves something  to be desired  fired
 

